1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,034
Tsutomu Yamaguchi is the only person in
history officially recognised as having

2
00:00:05,034 --> 00:00:07,742
survived both atomic bombs.

3
00:00:08,792 --> 00:00:14,292
Not nearby. Not in the next town. Three
kilometres from the centre of Hiroshima on

4
00:00:14,292 --> 00:00:18,912
the sixth of August, 1945, and three
kilometres from the centre of Nagasaki

5
00:00:18,912 --> 00:00:20,747
three days later.

6
00:00:21,197 --> 00:00:25,363
He was not a soldier or a scientist. He
was a twenty-nine-year-old engineer who

7
00:00:25,363 --> 00:00:29,574
designed oil tankers for a living. And the
only reason he was alive to be in Nagasaki

8
00:00:29,574 --> 00:00:33,691
at all is that on the morning of the
sixth, he turned back for a stamp.

9
00:00:33,741 --> 00:00:39,127
He had been in Hiroshima three months on a
business trip for Mitsubishi. That morning

10
00:00:39,127 --> 00:00:44,455
was his last. He was walking to the tram
with his bags when he realised he had left

11
00:00:44,455 --> 00:00:50,013
his personal seal at the office. The small
carved stamp that, in Japan in 1945,

12
00:00:50,013 --> 00:00:56,028
served as a man's signature. Without it,
no travel papers were valid. He could not

13
00:00:56,028 --> 00:00:57,467
get home.

14
00:00:57,517 --> 00:01:00,562
So he turned around and walked back for
it.

15
00:01:01,112 --> 00:01:05,198
That walk put him in a field on the edge
of the city at fifteen minutes past eight

16
00:01:05,198 --> 00:01:09,286
in the morning, instead of on a tram
heading into the centre.

17
00:01:09,336 --> 00:01:14,905
He noticed the aircraft first. A single
bomber, very high up. Then something small

18
00:01:14,905 --> 00:01:16,808
falling beneath it.

19
00:01:18,058 --> 00:01:19,781
Then the sky went white.

20
00:01:22,281 --> 00:01:27,444
Three days later, four hundred kilometres
away, in the middle of a sentence, it

21
00:01:27,444 --> 00:01:28,863
happened again.

22
00:01:30,863 --> 00:01:35,907
Yamaguchi was born in Nagasaki in March
1916, and by 1945 he had the kind of life

23
00:01:35,907 --> 00:01:40,774
that does not end up in history books. A
draughtsman at Mitsubishi Heavy

24
00:01:40,774 --> 00:01:47,361
Industries. A wife, Hisako, and an infant
son. A man who drew ships for a living and

25
00:01:47,361 --> 00:01:49,729
went home to his family.

26
00:01:49,779 --> 00:01:54,919
In the summer of 1945 Mitsubishi sent him
to Hiroshima for three months. He

27
00:01:54,919 --> 00:02:00,549
travelled with two colleagues, Akira
Iwanaga and Kuniyoshi Sato. They finished

28
00:02:00,549 --> 00:02:06,162
the work, and on the morning of August the
sixth they were due to leave.

29
00:02:06,212 --> 00:02:10,567
The seal he went back for is worth
understanding, because the whole story

30
00:02:10,567 --> 00:02:16,279
turns on it. A hanko is a small cylinder,
usually wood or ivory, with a family name

31
00:02:16,279 --> 00:02:21,991
carved into one end. Pressed into red ink,
it functions as a signature, and in 1945

32
00:02:21,991 --> 00:02:27,873
it was not optional. Travel documents,
company paperwork, bank forms. None of

33
00:02:27,873 --> 00:02:30,316
them were valid without it.

34
00:02:30,366 --> 00:02:35,678
So he walked back. That detour is the
reason anyone knows his name.

35
00:02:36,528 --> 00:02:41,191
At fifteen minutes past eight, a B-29
called the Enola Gay released a uranium

36
00:02:41,191 --> 00:02:46,247
bomb over the centre of Hiroshima. It
detonated roughly six hundred metres above

37
00:02:46,247 --> 00:02:51,247
the ground. The yield was about fifteen
kilotons, the equivalent of fifteen

38
00:02:51,247 --> 00:02:56,528
thousand tons of high explosive. Around a
hundred and forty thousand people would be

39
00:02:56,528 --> 00:02:59,013
dead by the end of the year.

40
00:02:59,063 --> 00:03:03,889
Yamaguchi was about three kilometres from
the point directly beneath it.

41
00:03:04,739 --> 00:03:09,879
He described seeing the aircraft, and then
two small objects drifting down. Then the

42
00:03:09,879 --> 00:03:15,072
light. He said it was like a magnesium
flare going off in front of his face. He

43
00:03:15,072 --> 00:03:19,841
dropped to the ground. The blast wave
lifted him, threw him, and he lost

44
00:03:19,841 --> 00:03:22,363
consciousness for a short time.

45
00:03:22,413 --> 00:03:27,425
When he came round, it was dark. Not
night. The sky had filled with dust and

46
00:03:27,425 --> 00:03:32,232
smoke thick enough to blot out the
morning. His left side was badly burned.

47
00:03:32,232 --> 00:03:36,366
Both eardrums had ruptured. He was
temporarily blinded.

48
00:03:36,416 --> 00:03:41,737
He got up and walked toward what was left
of the city, looking for his colleagues.

49
00:03:41,737 --> 00:03:46,721
What he walked through has been described
many times by many survivors, and there is

50
00:03:46,721 --> 00:03:51,664
no version of it that is easy to hear. He
found both men alive.

51
00:03:51,714 --> 00:03:56,054
And then he did the thing that makes this
story what it is.

52
00:03:56,904 --> 00:03:59,247
He decided to go home.

53
00:04:00,697 --> 00:04:05,368
He spent that first night in an air-raid
shelter with other survivors. Nobody in

54
00:04:05,368 --> 00:04:10,091
the shelter knew what had happened. There
had been no siren worth the name, no

55
00:04:10,091 --> 00:04:15,891
formation of bombers, no sustained raid.
One aircraft, one flash, and a city gone.

56
00:04:15,891 --> 00:04:20,357
People tried to explain it to each other
with the only words they had, and none of

57
00:04:20,357 --> 00:04:21,939
the words fit.

58
00:04:21,989 --> 00:04:26,890
Nobody in Hiroshima knew what an atomic
bomb was. The term did not exist in their

59
00:04:26,890 --> 00:04:32,064
vocabulary that morning. Yamaguchi had
been burned by a weapon that, as far as he

60
00:04:32,064 --> 00:04:34,483
understood, had no name.

61
00:04:35,033 --> 00:04:38,834
The next day he and his colleagues set out
for the station.

62
00:04:38,884 --> 00:04:43,770
The trains were, improbably, still
running. Hiroshima's rail links had been

63
00:04:43,770 --> 00:04:48,497
damaged but not destroyed, and in the days
after the bombing people left the city in

64
00:04:48,497 --> 00:04:54,126
enormous numbers. For relatives, for
hospitals, for anywhere else. To reach the

65
00:04:54,126 --> 00:04:58,884
station Yamaguchi had to cross the river.
The bridges were down.

66
00:04:58,934 --> 00:05:00,872
He crossed on what was in the water.

67
00:05:03,072 --> 00:05:07,952
The journey to Nagasaki is a little over
four hundred kilometres. Today it takes

68
00:05:07,952 --> 00:05:12,887
about two hours. In August 1945 it took
the better part of a day, on crowded

69
00:05:12,887 --> 00:05:17,349
trains, through a country that was running
out of everything.

70
00:05:17,399 --> 00:05:21,778
He made that journey with his left side
burned raw, his arms wrapped in whatever

71
00:05:21,778 --> 00:05:26,366
cloth he had been given, deaf in both
ears, travelling through a landscape that

72
00:05:26,366 --> 00:05:32,517
looked entirely normal. Fields. Stations.
Other passengers. And he carried in his

73
00:05:32,517 --> 00:05:37,507
head something that nobody else on that
train had any way of understanding yet.

74
00:05:37,557 --> 00:05:42,697
He reached Nagasaki late and went to a
hospital, where a doctor he knew, a former

75
00:05:42,697 --> 00:05:48,125
classmate, treated his burns. By the
accounts given later, his face and arms

76
00:05:48,125 --> 00:05:53,322
were so heavily bandaged that people who
knew him did not recognise him. He went

77
00:05:53,322 --> 00:05:59,447
home. His wife, Hisako, thought at first
that she was looking at a ghost.

78
00:05:59,497 --> 00:06:02,758
He slept at home on the night of August
the eighth.

79
00:06:03,308 --> 00:06:08,255
And on the morning of August the ninth,
badly burned, deaf, bandaged from the

80
00:06:08,255 --> 00:06:12,940
shoulders up, Tsutomu Yamaguchi got
dressed and went in to work.

81
00:06:14,190 --> 00:06:19,101
It is easy to make that sound like
foolishness. It was not. In Japan in

82
00:06:19,101 --> 00:06:24,730
August 1945, you went to work. The country
was in its fourth year of total war, food

83
00:06:24,730 --> 00:06:30,359
was rationed, cities were being firebombed
nightly, and absence had to be explained.

84
00:06:30,359 --> 00:06:35,270
He was a Mitsubishi employee who had been
away three months on company business, and

85
00:06:35,270 --> 00:06:39,428
he had to report what had happened to the
Hiroshima office.

86
00:06:39,478 --> 00:06:44,845
So he walked into the Mitsubishi shipyard
offices in Nagasaki and sat down in front

87
00:06:44,845 --> 00:06:47,005
of his supervisor.

88
00:06:47,855 --> 00:06:52,725
The supervisor's name was Sato. He
listened to Yamaguchi describe what had

89
00:06:52,725 --> 00:06:58,650
happened in Hiroshima. One aircraft, one
bomb, and a city of three hundred thousand

90
00:06:58,650 --> 00:07:01,699
people destroyed in a single moment.

91
00:07:01,749 --> 00:07:05,495
And he told Yamaguchi he was out of his
mind.

92
00:07:06,345 --> 00:07:12,049
It is worth being fair to the man. What he
was being told was, by every standard he

93
00:07:12,049 --> 00:07:17,871
had, impossible. He was looking at an
employee who was burned, deaf, heavily

94
00:07:17,871 --> 00:07:22,743
bandaged and visibly in shock, describing
something no bomb in the history of

95
00:07:22,743 --> 00:07:28,744
warfare had ever done. He asked Yamaguchi,
reasonably, from where he sat, how a

96
00:07:28,744 --> 00:07:32,124
single bomb could destroy an entire city.

97
00:07:32,174 --> 00:07:36,758
Yamaguchi was still trying to explain when
the room filled with white light.

98
00:07:39,758 --> 00:07:44,736
At two minutes past eleven on the morning
of August the ninth, 1945, a B-29 called

99
00:07:44,736 --> 00:07:49,823
Bockscar dropped a plutonium bomb over the
Urakami valley in Nagasaki. The yield was

100
00:07:49,823 --> 00:07:55,018
higher than Hiroshima's, around twenty-one
kilotons. The hills around the valley

101
00:07:55,018 --> 00:08:00,051
contained the blast, which is the only
reason the death toll was lower. Roughly

102
00:08:00,051 --> 00:08:02,890
seventy thousand people were killed.

103
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:07,659
Yamaguchi was about three kilometres from
the hypocentre. Again.

104
00:08:08,709 --> 00:08:13,480
The office windows blew in. He was thrown
to the floor for the second time in three

105
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:18,095
days, by the second atomic weapon ever
used against human beings, while standing

106
00:08:18,095 --> 00:08:22,283
in a room trying to convince someone that
the first one existed.

107
00:08:22,333 --> 00:08:27,083
His bandages were torn away in the blast.
He got up, and this time he knew exactly

108
00:08:27,083 --> 00:08:28,833
what had happened.

109
00:08:29,383 --> 00:08:34,567
Hisako and their infant son, Katsutoshi,
had been at home when the bomb went off.

110
00:08:34,567 --> 00:08:39,381
Yamaguchi went to find them. He searched
the ruins of the Urakami district street

111
00:08:39,381 --> 00:08:42,958
by street, not knowing whether they were
alive.

112
00:08:43,008 --> 00:08:47,188
They were. Hisako had taken the baby out
that morning to look for burn ointment,

113
00:08:47,188 --> 00:08:51,862
for her husband's injuries from Hiroshima.
Being out of the house is what saved them.

114
00:08:51,862 --> 00:08:55,178
They were caught in black rain, but they
were alive.

115
00:08:55,228 --> 00:08:59,296
His injuries from the first bomb are the
reason his wife and son survived the

116
00:08:59,296 --> 00:09:00,406
second one.

117
00:09:02,606 --> 00:09:05,003
Six days later, the war ended.

118
00:09:06,253 --> 00:09:11,067
Most versions of this story end at
Nagasaki, as though the remarkable part

119
00:09:11,067 --> 00:09:17,343
were the surviving. The remarkable part is
the sixty-five years afterwards.

120
00:09:17,393 --> 00:09:22,382
He was ill for a long time. His hair fell
out. His burns took years to heal

121
00:09:22,382 --> 00:09:26,863
properly. He was deaf in one ear for the
rest of his life.

122
00:09:26,913 --> 00:09:32,137
And then he did something very ordinary.
He worked as a translator for the American

123
00:09:32,137 --> 00:09:37,644
occupation forces in Nagasaki. Later he
taught at a junior high school. In the

124
00:09:37,644 --> 00:09:42,925
early 1950s he and Hisako had two
daughters. He returned to Mitsubishi. He

125
00:09:42,925 --> 00:09:44,808
drew ships again.

126
00:09:44,858 --> 00:09:50,433
In 1957 the Japanese government formally
recognised the survivors of the bombings,

127
00:09:50,433 --> 00:09:56,590
the hibakusha, and issued them
certificates. Yamaguchi's named Nagasaki.

128
00:09:56,590 --> 00:10:00,700
Only Nagasaki. Hiroshima was not on it.

129
00:10:00,750 --> 00:10:05,900
He did not correct it. Not for fifty
years.

130
00:10:06,950 --> 00:10:11,635
Part of the reason was practical. Being
certified for one bombing already entitled

131
00:10:11,635 --> 00:10:15,908
him to medical care and an allowance, and
adding the second changed nothing on

132
00:10:15,908 --> 00:10:20,953
paper. But the larger reason, by his own
account, was that he felt comparatively

133
00:10:20,953 --> 00:10:26,409
well. He had survived. He had raised
children. There were people far worse off

134
00:10:26,409 --> 00:10:30,569
than he was, and he did not think his case
needed attention.

135
00:10:30,619 --> 00:10:35,856
There is also something people outside
Japan tend not to know. Being hibakusha

136
00:10:35,856 --> 00:10:41,997
carried a stigma. Survivors were treated
as contaminated. Refused jobs, refused

137
00:10:41,997 --> 00:10:47,956
marriages, suspected of passing something
on to their children. For decades, many

138
00:10:47,956 --> 00:10:54,507
said nothing at all. The silence was not
modesty. It was self-protection.

139
00:10:54,557 --> 00:10:58,195
So he stayed quiet about it until he was
nearly ninety.

140
00:10:58,745 --> 00:11:04,294
What changed, by most accounts, was his
son. Katsutoshi, the baby his wife had

141
00:11:04,294 --> 00:11:09,282
carried out of the house on the morning of
the ninth, died of cancer in 2005, at

142
00:11:09,282 --> 00:11:15,616
fifty-nine. Hisako died in 2008, of liver
and kidney cancer, at eighty-eight. Both

143
00:11:15,616 --> 00:11:18,152
had been exposed at Nagasaki.

144
00:11:18,202 --> 00:11:21,786
After that, Yamaguchi began to talk.

145
00:11:22,636 --> 00:11:27,189
In his eighties he wrote a book about what
had happened to him. He took part in a

146
00:11:27,189 --> 00:11:31,540
documentary about niju hibakusha, double
survivors, which was screened at the

147
00:11:31,540 --> 00:11:36,700
United Nations, and he travelled there to
speak himself. He gave interviews. He

148
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:40,692
wrote tanka, short Japanese poems, about
the bombings.

149
00:11:40,742 --> 00:11:46,136
In March 2009, at ninety-three, Nagasaki
city officials certified him for Hiroshima

150
00:11:46,136 --> 00:11:51,357
as well. He became the only person on
record officially recognised as a survivor

151
00:11:51,357 --> 00:11:52,751
of both.

152
00:11:52,801 --> 00:11:57,843
He was asked why it mattered so late. He
said that having it in the official record

153
00:11:57,843 --> 00:12:02,557
meant it could still tell younger people
what the bombings had been, after he was

154
00:12:02,557 --> 00:12:03,729
gone.

155
00:12:03,779 --> 00:12:09,611
He said something else, too. What he hated
about the atomic bomb, he said, was what

156
00:12:09,611 --> 00:12:12,709
it does to the dignity of human beings.

157
00:12:14,159 --> 00:12:20,334
An estimated 165 people experienced both
bombings. He is the only one the record

158
00:12:20,334 --> 00:12:21,794
names.

159
00:12:22,644 --> 00:12:27,355
Tsutomu Yamaguchi died of stomach cancer
in Nagasaki on the fourth of January,

160
00:12:27,355 --> 00:12:30,630
2010. He was ninety-three years old.

161
00:12:33,030 --> 00:12:37,968
There is a version of this story that gets
told as a piece of trivia. The unluckiest

162
00:12:37,968 --> 00:12:43,012
man in the world, or the luckiest,
depending on who is telling it. It usually

163
00:12:43,012 --> 00:12:46,388
runs about ninety seconds and ends at
Nagasaki.

164
00:12:46,438 --> 00:12:48,943
That version is not worth much.

165
00:12:49,493 --> 00:12:54,068
What happened to Tsutomu Yamaguchi was not
luck in either direction. He was in

166
00:12:54,068 --> 00:12:58,591
Hiroshima because a company sent him
there. He was in a field instead of the

167
00:12:58,591 --> 00:13:03,376
city centre because he had forgotten a
stamp. He was in Nagasaki three days later

168
00:13:03,376 --> 00:13:07,846
because it was his home and he went back
to it, and because in August 1945 a man

169
00:13:07,846 --> 00:13:12,193
who had been away three months on business
reported to his office.

170
00:13:12,243 --> 00:13:16,942
He spent the next sixty-five years not
talking about any of it. He worked. He

171
00:13:16,942 --> 00:13:22,512
taught. He raised daughters. He buried a
son. And at ninety-three, with almost no

172
00:13:22,512 --> 00:13:26,775
time left, he decided the record should
say what had happened, so that it would

173
00:13:26,775 --> 00:13:29,111
still be there once he was not.

174
00:13:30,361 --> 00:13:35,378
He went back for his seal because without
it, he could not go home.

